1
00:00:01,073 --> 00:00:12,370
Welcome to episode five of our U.S. states series. First, I clear the map so
we can study a fresh group.

2
00:00:13,035 --> 00:00:23,438
For our final episode, we explore eleven Western states: the northern Rockies,
the Four Corners group, Nevada and the three Pacific coast states.

3
00:00:24,099 --> 00:00:31,982
Let's start in the northern Rockies with Montana, Idaho and Wyoming, directly
west of the Plains states we studied last time.

4
00:00:34,522 --> 00:00:46,376
Montana is the broad state along the Canadian border, west of North Dakota.
Mountains dominate its west, while its eastern land opens onto the Great
Plains.

5
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:58,000
Its name comes from the Spanish word for mountain. Glacier National Park,
established in 1910, lies in Montana's western Rockies.

6
00:00:58,662 --> 00:01:07,358
Remember Montana above Wyoming, with Idaho along its irregular western edge.
Idaho is our next stop.

7
00:01:09,897 --> 00:01:20,601
Idaho is the state with a narrow northern panhandle below Canada and a wider
southern body. It sits between the Pacific states and Montana and Wyoming.

8
00:01:21,258 --> 00:01:29,315
The card points to Hells Canyon on the Oregon border. Carved by the Snake
River, it is North America's deepest river gorge.

9
00:01:29,965 --> 00:01:39,009
Use Idaho's bent outline as a memory hook: a narrow top and a broader bottom.
Wyoming is to the southeast.

10
00:01:41,729 --> 00:01:53,455
Wyoming is the near-rectangular state directly below Montana and above
Colorado. Idaho is to its west, and the Dakotas and Nebraska are to its east.

11
00:01:54,116 --> 00:02:06,144
Wyoming Territory granted women voting rights in 1869. Yellowstone, much of it
in Wyoming, became the first U.S. national park in 1872.

12
00:02:06,812 --> 00:02:16,367
Remember this vertical Rocky Mountain stack: Montana, Wyoming, Colorado. We
now move south to Colorado and the Four Corners states.

13
00:02:17,116 --> 00:02:27,646
Our next view holds the Four Corners group: Colorado, New Mexico, Arizona and
Utah. We'll trace them clockwise around their shared meeting point.

14
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:41,198
Colorado is the broad rectangle below Wyoming and west of Kansas. Utah is to
its west, and New Mexico directly below it.

15
00:02:41,863 --> 00:02:54,100
Its Spanish name means coloured red, referring to the Colorado River. The
card's elevation fact is striking: its lowest point is higher than the highest
point in eighteen other states.

16
00:02:54,760 --> 00:03:01,157
Keep Colorado at the northeastern corner of our group. New Mexico is
immediately south, beginning our clockwise loop.

17
00:03:04,262 --> 00:03:13,817
New Mexico lies south of Colorado and east of Arizona. Texas borders it to the
east and south; a short southern stretch touches Mexico.

18
00:03:14,474 --> 00:03:25,689
The card recalls Trinity: the world's first atomic bomb test took place in New
Mexico in July 1945, after development work at Los Alamos.

19
00:03:26,351 --> 00:03:33,340
Remember New Mexico as the southeastern state of our Four Corners group.
Arizona is directly to its west.

20
00:03:36,487 --> 00:03:47,447
Arizona is west of New Mexico and south of Utah. Its southern border meets
Mexico, while its western edge follows the Colorado River in places.

21
00:03:48,099 --> 00:04:01,752
The Grand Canyon is Arizona's memory hook. The Colorado River carved this
landscape; the canyon extends about 277 miles, or 446 kilometres, along the
river.

22
00:04:02,408 --> 00:04:11,359
Keep Arizona below Utah and beside New Mexico. We now move north to Utah,
completing the loop.

23
00:04:13,693 --> 00:04:21,542
Utah sits north of Arizona and west of Colorado. Notice the notch in its
northeastern corner, with Wyoming beyond it.

24
00:04:22,205 --> 00:04:31,632
Its name is connected with the Ute people. The Great Salt Lake is the largest
natural lake west of the Mississippi, a memorable feature of northern Utah.

25
00:04:32,294 --> 00:04:43,509
Our Four Corners loop is Colorado, New Mexico, Arizona, Utah. Nevada lies west
of Utah and leads us toward the Pacific states.

26
00:04:44,192 --> 00:04:52,853
Now turn west to Nevada and California. We'll follow the inland mountains to
the Pacific, then travel north along the coast.

27
00:04:55,688 --> 00:05:05,626
Nevada lies between California and Utah, below Oregon and Idaho. Its long
diagonal western border fits against California.

28
00:05:06,286 --> 00:05:16,863
Its Spanish name means snow-covered, from the Sierra Nevada. Hoover Dam, on
the Arizona border, was built during the 1930s.

29
00:05:17,517 --> 00:05:25,145
Remember Nevada just inland from California. The angled border between them
makes a useful pair of matching outlines.

30
00:05:27,918 --> 00:05:36,962
California is the long Pacific state west of Nevada. Oregon is above it, and
Mexico below its southern border.

31
00:05:37,615 --> 00:05:52,580
Its name comes from a fictional island ruled by Queen Calafia. The state also
contains Mount Whitney and Death Valley, the highest and lowest points in the
contiguous United States.

32
00:05:53,233 --> 00:06:02,823
Use California's long coast and angled inland edge to remember its shape.
Next, follow that coast north into Oregon.

33
00:06:03,505 --> 00:06:13,606
Let's bring the Pacific Northwest closer. Oregon and Washington form our final
mainland pair, with the ocean to the west and Idaho inland.

34
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:25,173
Oregon is the Pacific state between California and Washington. Idaho is to the
east, across part of the Snake River boundary.

35
00:06:25,827 --> 00:06:36,148
Its name has an uncertain origin. Crater Lake, formed in the caldera of a
collapsed volcano, is the deepest lake in the United States.

36
00:06:36,803 --> 00:06:45,627
Remember the three-state Pacific coast chain: California, Oregon, Washington.
We move north to Washington next.

37
00:06:48,222 --> 00:06:57,777
Washington is the northwestern corner of the contiguous states. Canada is
above it, Oregon below it and Idaho to the east.

38
00:06:58,442 --> 00:07:11,747
It is the only state named for a U.S. president: George Washington. Mount St
Helens, also here, underwent its major landscape-changing eruption in 1980.

39
00:07:12,401 --> 00:07:21,956
Keep Washington State on the Pacific, far from Washington, D.C. Washington
completes our eleven Western states and the mainland series.

40
00:07:26,353 --> 00:07:39,658
Trace Montana, Idaho and Wyoming; then the Four Corners loop of Colorado, New
Mexico, Arizona and Utah. Nevada leads us to California, Oregon and
Washington.

41
00:07:40,318 --> 00:07:54,006
Across five videos, we have covered all forty-eight contiguous states, exactly
once. We deliberately left out Alaska and Hawaii: their distinctive locations
already make them easy to remember.

42
00:07:54,668 --> 00:08:05,419
Our mainland tour is complete. Use the regional groups, neighbouring states
and memorable facts to rebuild the map in your mind, then test yourself in
GeoClick.
